A||||12||86SE||Got the 'new' 3.4, now I have some questions that i cant find.||||14||
Z||000000||86SE||09-10-2001||12:05 AM||||Just picked up my 3.4 out of 94 Firebird this weekend.  The motor only has 11,000 miles and it came with everything, but flywheel/starter, alternator, a/c, and thats about it.  It has the ignition system on it, the EGR system, intake system with the throttle body, exhaust manifolds, and the water pump.<P>My questions are:  <BR>Can I use the water pump thats on the 3.4 if I put on the pully from my 2.8?<P>Can i use the Firebird ignition system?<P>And whats this I hear about needing an adapter for the oil pump?<P><BR>Thanks!<BR>Chuck<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg">     <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>V-6 w/rod knock Yellow Fiero<BR> -- 3.4L Conversion *Back in session!* --||||reg||14
Z||000001||86SE||09-10-2001||12:21 AM||||oh yeah, what do I have to do to the front engine mount?  Or can i just buy a different one?<P><BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg">     <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>V-6 w/rod knock Yellow Fiero<BR> -- 3.4L Conversion *Back in session!* --||||reg||1
Z||000002||Neal||09-10-2001||12:52 AM||||If you want the "common" 3.4 swap then strip the motor down to the long block and redress it with the fiero stuff.  But swap the fuel injectors.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||MontanaFiero||09-10-2001||01:52 AM||||Yeah, what Neal said-- change EVERYTHING, including the timing cover, and oil pan. Basically disguise it as a Fiero "original". The Fierbird waterpump is different and won't bolt up to the Fiero timing cover, and if you keep the Firebird timing cover and waterpump it will be too "thick" and the pulleys will hit the firewall.<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Don't use the Firebird flywheel. Go to the boneyard and get a 1988 (fiero) 2.8L flywheel or one out of a 90 3.1L front wheel drive.<P><LI>The harmonic balancer will need to be re- marked for timing<P><LI>You will need to re-drill the starter holes on the other side.<P><LI>Use all of your Fiero engine mounts. Some minor bracket modification is necessary if you have a/c (mainly tack-welding a bolt in one of the holes).<P><LI>Don't bother using the Firebird ignition or engine controls, you can use the Fiero distributor (look at the new motor and you will see where the distributor goes) <P><LI>As for the intake, notice that it faces the wrong direction.<BR></UL><BR>Be prepared to replace most of your "worn out" Fiero engine controls and sensors (i.e. IAC, O<sub>2</sub> sensor, temp sensors) -- and about $120 worth in gaskets.<P>Email me if you have any specific questions, I am putting mine in just as soon as I get the drill guide in the mail to re-locate my starter.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTMike||09-10-2001||02:51 AM||||ok whats this about a drill guy.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  where did you get it????   i'm very intrested cause i'm going to be doing the swap around christmass.   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||MontanaFiero||09-10-2001||02:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTMike:<BR><B>ok whats this about a drill guy.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  where did you get it????   i'm very intrested cause i'm going to be doing the swap around christmass.   </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's  a drill <B>guide</B>, not a guy...lol...you can rent it  from the fiero shop. <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net</A> <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
